 Then point Chromium at `https://login.example.com`. With a self-signed
 certificate, add `--ignore-certificate-errors` for testing.
 
+## Accessing from another machine
+
+`--host` is only the **bind address**. `--public-url` is the origin that ends
+up in cookies, in `id_token.iss` and in the status page, so it must be an
+address the client can actually reach.
+
+Three values must be the same origin:
+
+1. `--public-url`
+2. the `--url` you pass to `gen-gaia-config`
+3. the address the browser connects to
+
+Example for a machine with LAN address `192.168.1.23`:
+
+```sh
+ip -4 addr show | grep 'inet '        # find the reachable address
+
+cd tools/afree-login-service
+node src/server.mjs --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8080 \
+  --public-url http://192.168.1.23:8080
+
+# in another shell, generate the client config for the same origin
+node src/cli.mjs gen-gaia-config --url http://192.168.1.23:8080 \
+  > gaia-config.json
+
+# verify from the client machine
+curl http://192.168.1.23:8080/healthz
+```
+
+Open the firewall if needed:
+
+```sh
+sudo ufw allow 8080/tcp
+# or
+sudo firewall-cmd --add-port=8080/tcp --permanent && sudo firewall-cmd --reload
+```
+
+If this machine is a VM behind NAT, also forward the port on the hypervisor
+(host `8080` -> guest `8080`), and use the address/hostname the client can see.
+
+Run it detached:
+
+```sh
+nohup node src/server.mjs --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8080 \
+  --public-url http://192.168.1.23:8080 > server.log 2>&1 &
+```
+
+Across the Internet, terminate TLS in front (nginx, Caddy, …) and use the
+public hostname, then regenerate the client config with the same URL:
+
+```sh
+node src/server.mjs --public-url https://login.example.com
+node src/cli.mjs gen-gaia-config --url https://login.example.com \
+  > gaia-config.json
+```
+
+Cookies are host-only, so `http://127.0.0.1:8080` and
+`http://192.168.1.23:8080` are different registrations: if you already signed
+in on one origin, switching to the other requires signing in again.
+
